1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:00,479
Hi!

2
00:00:00,479 --> 00:00:05,438
These are my two cats, Qb and Qbo, and they look so alike that my camera can't tell

3
00:00:05,438 --> 00:00:06,095
them apart.

4
00:00:06,375 --> 00:00:10,270
I'm at work all day, and I want to know whether they ate, slept, and played,

5
00:00:10,270 --> 00:00:13,755
because cats hide illness and small changes in their routine matter.

6
00:00:14,035 --> 00:00:15,671
But my camera makes mistakes.

7
00:00:15,671 --> 00:00:19,844
Last week, it decided this exercise ball was Qbo, and it believed that for

8
00:00:19,844 --> 00:00:20,916
twenty-six minutes.

9
00:00:21,196 --> 00:00:23,575
So why not send every frame to a big AI model?

10
00:00:23,575 --> 00:00:27,765
We tried it, and eight minutes of video took an hour and twenty-five dollars, and

11
00:00:27,765 --> 00:00:29,369
it still missed a sleeping cat.

12
00:00:29,649 --> 00:00:34,122
A bigger model on the device won't fit either, because a Raspberry Pi only runs

13
00:00:34,122 --> 00:00:36,500
YOLO nano, at about three frames a second.

14
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:39,275
That model is almost free, but it makes mistakes.

15
00:00:39,555 --> 00:00:44,104
Training a larger detector is out of scope for us, so we keep the small model and

16
00:00:44,104 --> 00:00:45,451
make it smarter with AI.

17
00:00:45,451 --> 00:00:46,406
So we built both.

18
00:00:46,686 --> 00:00:49,211
We call it Agentic Realtime Computer Vision.

19
00:00:49,211 --> 00:00:52,999
The AI gives the camera a brain, and the camera gives the AI eyes.

20
00:00:53,279 --> 00:00:54,347
Here's how it works.

21
00:00:54,347 --> 00:00:58,777
The Pi watches every frame, and when it's unsure, our bridge asks Meta's Muse Spark

22
00:00:58,777 --> 00:01:01,233
about that moment and remembers what's normal.

23
00:01:01,233 --> 00:01:03,582
Only those few moments ever reach the agent.

24
00:01:03,862 --> 00:01:05,003
Those doubts are rare.

25
00:01:05,003 --> 00:01:09,099
In eight minutes of footage, the Pi raised only twelve, and it handled the last

26
00:01:09,099 --> 00:01:10,914
five minutes completely on its own.

27
00:01:11,194 --> 00:01:15,437
Each doubt has a name, and there are fifteen kinds, like "I relabeled this cat by

28
00:01:15,437 --> 00:01:16,275
distance alone."

29
00:01:16,555 --> 00:01:20,753
Every doubt then becomes a plain-English question, with its evidence and a strict

30
00:01:20,753 --> 00:01:21,478
answer format.

31
00:01:21,758 --> 00:01:23,422
The agent does the work itself.

32
00:01:23,422 --> 00:01:27,554
It pulls the frames it needs, zooms into each cat, and answers with a reason.

33
00:01:27,554 --> 00:01:30,398
In this call, it settled four related doubts at once.

34
00:01:30,678 --> 00:01:34,962
Related doubts are reviewed together, so twelve questions took only five calls.

35
00:01:35,242 --> 00:01:39,126
Each verdict rewrites the record, and every change points back to its trace.

36
00:01:39,406 --> 00:01:42,265
The system also keeps a memory, written as a diary.

37
00:01:42,265 --> 00:01:46,469
For example, Qb slept in the green bed from four thirty-four to four forty.

38
00:01:46,749 --> 00:01:50,694
Over the weeks, it learns what normal looks like for each cat, in each spot.

39
00:01:50,694 --> 00:01:53,652
That way, every new question arrives with useful context.

40
00:01:53,932 --> 00:01:54,842
So does it work?

41
00:01:54,842 --> 00:01:59,275
Here, the tracker called the sleeping cat Qbo, but the agent saw white fur and

42
00:01:59,275 --> 00:02:01,265
white paws and overturned it to Qb.

43
00:02:01,545 --> 00:02:05,358
Here, two cats walked close together and the tracker swapped them, so the agent

44
00:02:05,358 --> 00:02:07,337
checked both and swapped them right back.

45
00:02:07,617 --> 00:02:11,853
And when the detector itself is wrong, the cat gets relabeled, and the ball is

46
00:02:11,853 --> 00:02:13,102
saved as a known decoy.

47
00:02:13,382 --> 00:02:16,536
Put together, the same eight minutes now tell the true story.

48
00:02:16,536 --> 00:02:19,276
Qb slept the whole time, while Qbo walked in and out.

49
00:02:19,556 --> 00:02:20,749
Here are the numbers.

50
00:02:20,749 --> 00:02:25,464
About one percent of frames call the AI, false alarms drop by ninety-three percent,

51
00:02:25,464 --> 00:02:29,725
and ninety-nine percent of what the camera saw gets the right cat, for just

52
00:02:29,725 --> 00:02:30,748
thirty-nine cents.

53
00:02:31,028 --> 00:02:35,727
Cats are just the start, because lab mice, tiny drones, cars in fog, and production

54
00:02:35,727 --> 00:02:37,312
lines all have the same gap.

55
00:02:37,312 --> 00:02:41,671
Any small model watching for rare moments could use a smarter second opinion.

56
00:02:41,951 --> 00:02:46,215
Next, we'll run Muse Glimmer locally, so your own agent can use these eyes and its

57
00:02:46,215 --> 00:02:47,567
verdicts can teach the Pi.

58
00:02:47,847 --> 00:02:52,096
This is Agentic Realtime Computer Vision, built this week on Meta's Muse Spark.

59
00:02:52,096 --> 00:02:53,602
Thanks so much for watching!

